      {
        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "2ce15c84-3327-5fd4-874a-bd767423b48b",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Pyrrhus of Epirus\n- Places: Pandosia, Heraclea, river Siris, Sinni, Epirus\n- Groups: Molossians, Aeacid house\n- Sources: Plutarch, Tabulae Heracleenses\n\nFacts:\n- Pyrrhus of Epirus was king of Epirus from 306 to 302 BC and again from 297 to 272 BC.\n- From about 297 BC, Pyrrhus of Epirus was king of the Molossians and belonged to the royal Aeacid house.\n- Plutarch states that Pyrrhus of Epirus encamped in the plain between Pandosia and Heraclea with the river Siris, identified as the modern Sinni, in front of him.\n- The Tabulae Heracleenses repeatedly refer to a town named Pandosia in the immediate neighborhood of Heraclea.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "d82df07a-cb45-56fe-aac5-b1c090de1df6",
        "text": "Page: Pandosia (Lucania). Sentence: Plutarch describes Pyrrhus as encamping in the plain between Pandosia and Heraclea, with the river Siris (modern Sinni) in front of him; and the celebrated \"Tabulae Heracleenses\" repeatedly refer to the existence of a town of the name in the immediate neighborhood of Heraclea.\nPage: Pyrrhus of Epirus. Sentence: He was king of the Greek tribe of Molossians, of the royal Aeacid house (from c. 297 BC), and later he became king of Epirus (r. 306–302, 297–272 BC).",
